    metadata: {}
  - kind: 1
    languageId: markdown
    value: As expected, the records on the dataset have some casing problem. Some
      data were CAPITALIZED, In Title Case, or, in small caps
    metadata: {}
  - kind: 1
    languageId: markdown
    value: ""
    metadata: {}
  - kind: 1
    languageId: markdown
    value: "## Normalize Table\r\n"
    metadata: {}
  - kind: 1
    languageId: markdown
    value: "the table is at around 60% correct, however there are violations in
      there in 3NF. hence, the reason to normalize this table. \r

      \r

      #### Why?\r

      \r

      Because the table already satisfies 1nf and 2nf.\r

      - Every cell is atomic (satisfies 1nf)\r

      - The table does not have a composite key (satisfies 2nf)\r

      \r

      \r

      In 3nf is where it gets interesting. Example, look at the `applicant_name`
      column. As you can see below, there multiple occurences of one name. "
    metadata: {}
  - kind: 2
    languageId: sql
    value: "SELECT COUNT(applicant_name) AS name_count\r

      FROM bronze_job_applicationss\r

      WHERE applicant_name = 'Jamie Santos';"
    metadata: {}
  - kind: 1
    languageId: markdown
    value: "The name `Jamie Santos` have 2 records on the table. Editing this
      specific name, would require you to edit multiple rows. And that would be
      inneficient.\r

      \r

      This also applies to other non-key detail columns, such as, recruiter,
      applicant details, company details, etc.\r

      \r

      This is a data anomaly. The current table suffers from update, insertion,
      and deletion anomaly because if you alter one row, you should also alter
      the row with the same identity on the one you are altering (e.g, altering
      `Jamie Santos` to a new name, would require you to edit also the other
      `Jamie Santos` on another row since they point to the same person. If not,
      you're basically adding new data to the table, hence, the anomaly!).\r

      \r

      To fix that, we must put these different entities to their own table, to
      be referenced later in the main application table.\r

      \ "
    metadata: {}
  - kind: 1
    languageId: markdown
    value: "## Next Approach\r

      \r

      - fix table datatypes\r

      - clean table (fill recruiter to `unknown`. Leave the other nulls as is)\r

      - normalize table"
    metadata: {}
  - kind: 1
    languageId: markdown
    value: ""
    metadata: {}
